<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daingean</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Philipstown</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daingean is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-offaly">County Offaly</a> in the Irish midlands, once the chief stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oconnor-faly">O'Connor Faly</a> lords. After the English took it in the 1540s, it was renamed Philipstown in 1556 after Philip II, husband of Mary I, and became the county town of the new King's County. It lost that role to Tullamore in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daingean.jpg" alt="Daingean" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Padraig McGrath · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MolesworthBridgeDaingean.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Daingean lies in the east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-offaly">County Offaly</a>, midway between Tullamore and Edenderry on the R402 road, which forms its Main Street. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-shannon">River Shannon</a>, passes through the village, and the Bog of Allen surrounds it. Its Irish name, Daingean Ua bhFáilghe, means the fortress of the Uí Fáilghe and comes from the medieval island fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oconnor-faly">O'Connor Faly</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town and the newly shired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-offaly">King's County</a>, planted under Mary I, took their names from her husband and co-monarch Philip, then titular King of Ireland, who lost that royal status at her death in 1558 and was soon crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>. The parliamentary borough of Philipstown sent two members to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-house-of-commons">Irish House of Commons</a> until it was abolished in 1801. Tullamore, which lay on a railway line, took over as the focus of the county in 1883, and Philipstown was reduced to a village. With the foundation of the Irish Free State in 1922 it was renamed Daingean, as King's County became County Offaly.</p><p>In the 1850s Philipstown Gaol held people sentenced to transportation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> while they waited for a ship, and many died there after years of waiting. The town's public buildings include a courthouse whose design is locally attributed to James Gandon, St Conleth's Reformatory for children, and the remains of a military barracks that gave its name to the footbarrack bridge towards Tullamore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Daingean had 1,223 inhabitants at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and is the principal town of the Catholic parish of Daingean. Bord Na Mona's work on the surrounding bog was long a main source of employment. A bog body known as Old Croghan Man was found near the town in 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daingean” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their stronghold at <b>Daingean</b>, set among bogs, was their main fortress in the 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ui-failge">Kingdom of Ui Failge</a></li></ul></section>
